UV-compatible aqueous processing and local cyanide concentration rather than deep submarine conditions; alkaline-lake ferrocyanide storage offers a plausible cyanide reservoir (Toner and Catling, 2019), and early Earth surface UV remained available (Ranjan and Sasselov, 2017)."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Alternative oxidative coupling to cyanoacetylene", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(I)", "aqueous": true, "other": "UV-compatible cyanide-rich surface waters with staged reagent supply"}, "mechanism": "Cu(I)-mediated oxidative coupling of HCN-derived carbon with acetylene yields the more unsaturated nitrile cyanoacetylene.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) reported efficient HCN-acetylene oxidative coupling to cyanoacetylene in a related branch of the cyanosulfidic network. It is included here as an alternative entry node because it is experimentally accessible from the same feedstocks, although its downstream use for arginine is less directly demonstrated than the acrylonitrile branch."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Partial hydrogenation of cyanoacetylene to acrylonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_014"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Ni"], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-370K", "pressure": "1-5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Ni", "aqueous": false, "other": "subaerial geothermal mineral interface or thin-film setting with episodic H2 exposure"}, "mechanism": "Heterogeneous partial hydrogenation reduces the alkyne of cyanoacetylene to the alkene, giving acrylonitrile.", "reasoning": "This exact cyanoacetylene-to-acrylonitrile step is speculative in the supplied arginine literature. It is included because Patel et al. (2015) make cyanoacetylene from the same feedstocks, and prebiotic H2/Ni reduction chemistry is at least chemically plausible in adjacent geochemical settings (Yoshino et al., 1971; Kaur et al., 2024). Selective partial hydrogenation, rather than over-reduction or polymerization, remains an unresolved risk."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Ammonia addition to acrylonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "ammonia-rich pool at moderate pH"}, "mechanism": "Aza-Michael addition of NH3 across the activated double bond of acrylonitrile forms beta-aminopropionitrile.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) identified beta-aminopropionitrile as the key branch-point intermediate for arginine/proline chemistry. No specific mineral catalyst is required in the reported chemistry beyond access to ammonia in aqueous surface systems."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Photoreduction of beta-aminopropionitrile to beta-aminopropionaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "UV irradiation with HS-/H2S photoredox; optional Cu photoredox acceleration; rapid downstream trapping favored"}, "mechanism": "H2S-driven UV photoreduction converts the nitrile branch-point to the corresponding aldehyde, beta-aminopropionaldehyde.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) specifically showed H2S/UV reduction of beta-aminopropionitrile to beta-aminopropionaldehyde, the stage at which cyanamide trapping becomes much more effective than direct aminonitrile guanidination. Surface UV availability is supported by Ranjan and Sasselov (2017), while sulfur availability is geochemically constrained and likely local rather than globally abundant (Ranjan et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Aldehyde-stage cyanosulfidic construction of arginine alpha-aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_005", "mol_001", "mol_004", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "UV irradiation; staged addition of cyanamide, HCN, H2S and NH3; optional Cu photoredox acceleration; flow or wet-dry cycling"}, "mechanism": "Cyanamide traps the aldehyde to a guanidinated aldehyde/hemiaminal manifold; HCN adds as cyanohydrin; H2S enables thioamide chemistry; repeated photoreduction and final NH3 addition furnish arginine alpha-aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "This is the strongest Arg-specific de novo branch. Patel et al. (2015) found aldehyde-stage capture by residual cyanamide to be markedly more effective than direct guanidination of beta-aminopropionitrile, and they structurally characterized the arginine alpha-aminonitrile precursor. The network is represented here as a collapsed multistep surface sequence because the published chemistry is staged rather than one-pot. Its geochemical plausibility depends on localized cyanide concentration and a workable sulfur/UV balance (Toner and Catling, 2019; Ranjan et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Direct beta-aminopropionitrile guanidination-homologation to arginine alpha-aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_005", "mol_001", "mol_004", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "UV irradiation; cyanamide present before photoreduction; staged HCN/H2S/NH3 inputs; optional Cu photoredox acceleration"}, "mechanism": "Beta-aminopropionitrile is first guanidinated by cyanamide, then carried through the same photoreductive homologation logic to the C5 arginine alpha-aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) reported direct cyanamide guanidination of beta-aminopropionitrile to be relatively inefficient, but not impossible. It is therefore included as a lower-efficiency alternative pathway. The later homologation to arginine alpha-aminonitrile is chemically inferred from the same cyanosulfidic logic, so this edge is less secure than the aldehyde-trapping route and should be treated as partially speculative."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Warm surface hydrolysis of arginine alpha-aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-360K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "wet-dry cycles or prolonged warm aqueous residence"}, "mechanism": "Stepwise nitrile hydration and hydrolysis converts the alpha-aminonitrile to the corresponding amino acid.", "reasoning": "Hydrolysis of alpha-aminonitriles to amino acids is the expected Strecker endpoint, so free arginine is the logical product of arginine alpha-aminonitrile hydrolysis. However, Patel et al. (2015) emphasized the aminonitrile precursor rather than a quantitative isolation of free arginine; this completion step is chemically expected but not directly established in the provided arginine literature and is therefore modestly speculative."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Compartmental aqueous hydrolysis of arginine alpha-aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "long residence in concentrated prebiotic pools or protocell-like compartments"}, "mechanism": "Slow aqueous nitrile hydration and hydrolysis proceeds under concentrated, retained conditions rather than open-surface dilution.", "reasoning": "This alternative endpoint keeps the final hydrolysis in a concentrated biochemical-style compartment rather than a surface wet-dry setting. The chemistry remains speculative because the supplied literature secures arginine alpha-aminonitrile more strongly than free arginine itself (Patel et al., 2015), but such compartmental retention is a 