e this framework as an ornithine source rather than a secure direct arginine route, because ornithine was confirmed whereas arginine was not on the confirmed list. The pathway remains low-yield and mechanistically unresolved."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Net protometabolic conversion of alpha-ketoglutarate to ornithine", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_003", "mol_014"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ni-bearing mineral", "meteoritic Fe-Ni"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295-320K", "pressure": "1-5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Ni-bearing mineral or meteoritic Fe-Ni", "aqueous": true, "other": "pH about 8; mineral-interface protometabolic chemistry; hidden glutamate-like intermediate not explicitly shown"}, "mechanism": "Net reductive amination first accesses glutamate-like chemistry and then, in a more speculative continuation, reduces and aminates the distal carboxyl functionality to the ornithine side chain.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) strongly supports the first part of this net transformation by showing H2/NH3/Ni conversion of alpha-ketoacids, including alpha-ketoglutarate, to glutamate under mild aqueous conditions. The continuation to ornithine has not been demonstrated and is explicitly identified as a major gap in the literature. This edge therefore deliberately departs from the literature by collapsing the supported alpha-ketoglutarate-to-glutamate step and the unresolved glutamate-to-ornithine bridge into one speculative net reaction for network compactness."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Direct acrylonitrile entry with aldehyde-stage trapping and surface hydrolysis", "description": "Highest-confidence Patel-style route in which acrylonitrile is formed directly, reduced to the aldehyde, trapped by cyanamide, elaborated to arginine alpha-aminonitrile, then hydrolyzed in warm surface waters.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_008