trile branch point.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) explicitly convert acrylonitrile to beta-aminopropionitrile by ammonia addition. No extra mineral assumption is needed here beyond aqueous ammonia availability, so the simplest chemically justified encoding is a direct solution-phase addition.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu-free H2S/UV reduction of beta-aminopropionitrile to beta-aminopropionaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV irradiation; HS-/H2S photoreduction; staged after aminonitrile formation; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sulfide-enabled photoreductive partial hydrolysis converts the nitrile to the corresponding aldehyde with sulfur oxidation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) show H2S/UV conversion of beta-aminopropionitrile to beta-aminopropionaldehyde. This Cu-free variant is retained because the broader cyanosulfidic system can use sulfide photochemistry without requiring copper at every step, although realistic sulfidic anion availability remains a geochemical constraint (Patel et al., 2015; Ranjan et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu-assisted H2S/UV reduction of beta-aminopropionitrile to beta-aminopropionaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/Cu(II)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV irradiation; HS-/H2S present; copper-enabled photoredox cycling; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The same sulfide-driven nitrile-to-aldehyde conversion is accelerated by Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox mediation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Copper photoredox accelerates several UV-driven reductions in the cyanosulfidic network, so a Cu-assisted version is chemically justified as an alternative to the slower Cu-free branch (Patel et al., 2015). The same surface constraints on UV availability and sulfur supply apply (Ranjan et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanamide trapping of beta-aminopropionaldehyde to 3-guanidinopropanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Freshly generated amino aldehyde intercepted by residual cyanamide; concentrated pool or wet-dry pulse; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The terminal amine of the amino aldehyde reacts with cyanamide to install the guanidino functionality at the aldehyde stage.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) specifically found aldehyde-stage cyanamide trapping to be more effective than direct guanidination of beta-aminopropionitrile, making this the best-supported branch toward arginine. Cyanamide persistence in water is chemically plausible on prebiotic timescales even though it also participates in competing reactions (Patel et al., 2015; Steinman et al., 1964).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu-free cyanosulfidic homologation of the guanidinated C3 aldehyde to arginine alpha-aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Net node represents staged use of two cyanide and two sulfide equivalents across cyanohydrin and thioamide chemistry; UV irradiation; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net Patel-style sequence of cyanohydrin formation, thioamide generation, UV-driven deoxygenation/reduction, and further homologation to the C5 alpha-aminonitrile precursor.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This node compresses the experimentally supported Patel arginine branch from the guanidinated C3 aldehyde through cyanohydrin and thioamide intermediates to the arginine alpha-aminonitrile precursor, which Patel et al. (2015) explicitly characterize. The Cu-free variant reflects a slower but chemically defensible HS-/UV route; the literature still lacks an end-to-end environmental mass balance to free arginine itself (Patel et al., 2015; Ranjan et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu-assisted cyanosulfidic homologation of the guanidinated C3 aldehyde to arginine alpha-aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/Cu(II)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Net node represents staged use of two cyanide and two sulfide equivalents; UV irradiation; copper-assisted photoredox; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Copper-accelerated version of the Patel homologation sequence converts the guanidinated C3 aldehyde into the arginine alpha-aminonitrile precursor.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) show that Cu photoredox can accelerate related cyanosulfidic reductions, so a Cu-assisted homologation branch is chemically justified. The secure experimental endpoint remains the alpha-aminonitrile precursor rather than isolated free arginine, so this reaction is strong for precursor formation but not a full free-Arg demonstration (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface hydrolysis of arginine alpha-aminonitrile to arginine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Warm hydration during geothermal or evaporative cycling; prolonged contact with water; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential nitrile hydration and hydrolysis convert the alpha-aminonitrile to the amino acid with ammonia release.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydrolysis of an alpha-aminonitrile to the amino acid is the expected Strecker endpoint, so arginine formation from the Patel precursor is chemically reasonable. However, Patel et al. (2015) do not quantify free arginine as the endpoint, so this surface hydrolysis step is chemically plausible but still partly speculative for Arg specifically.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Compartmental hydrolysis of arginine alpha-aminonitrile to arginine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated prebiotic pool, vesicle lumen, or mineral-confined compartment with heating-cooling cycles; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The same nitrile-to-amino-acid hydrolysis occurs after concentration in a confined aqueous microenvironment.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"A concentrated prebiotic pool or compartment provides an alternative hydrolytic environment for the same aminonitrile-to-amino-acid conversion. This is again chemically reasonable but not directly measured as the terminating step of the Patel arginine branch, so it is retained as a lower-confidence endpoint (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Speculative cyanosulfidic homologation of beta-aminopropionaldehyde to ornithine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Net node represents staged use of two cyanide and two sulfide equivalents; wet-dry or flow-through cycling; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"By analogy to cyanosulfidic homologation, the C3 amino aldehyde undergoes iterative cyanide-based chain extension and reduction-hydrolysis to a C5 diamino acid skeleton.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative. No supplied study demonstrates beta-aminopropionaldehyde to ornithine under one prebiotic protocol. It is included because Patel et al. (2015) establish iterative HCN/H2S-based C-chain extension logic from related C3 nodes, while independent abiotic ornithine occurrence is supported in volcanic-discharge material (Johnson et al., 2008). This edge should be treated as a plausible bridge, not an experimentally established route.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Aqueous guanidination of ornithine by cyanamide to arginine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated aqueous pool or compartment; mildly alkaline to neutral; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The terminal delta-amine of ornithine attacks cyanamide, and proton transfer-tautomerization installs the guanidino group of arginine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The late-stage chemistry is strong: cyanamide is a simple plausible guanidinating reagent (Steinman et al., 1964), and Longo et al. (2020) showed complete aqueous Orn-to-Arg conversion for ornithine residues in model peptides. The key caveat is context: the strongest evidence is peptide-bound rather than a fully naturalistic free-monomer pond simulation, so environmental free-arginine yield remains unresolved.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Patel branch with Cu-free aldehyde formation, Cu-free homologation, and surface hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Most conservative Patel-style route: sulfide photochemistry without copper at the nitrile-to-aldehyde step, no copper in the downstream homologation node, and final hydrolysis in a warm surface pool.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Patel branch with Cu-free aldehyde formation, Cu-free homologation, and compartment hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Same Cu-free surface precursor chemistry as P1, but the aminonitrile is hydrolyzed after concentration in a biochemical-style pool or compartment.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\",