ated alkene nitrile to give the beta-amino nitrile branch point.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) explicitly convert acrylonitrile to beta-aminopropionitrile by ammonia addition. No extra mineral assumption is needed here beyond aqueous ammonia availability, so the simplest chemically justified encoding is a direct solution-phase addition."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Cu-free H2S/UV reduction of beta-aminopropionitrile to beta-aminopropionaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_007", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_003", "mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "UV irradiation; HS-/H2S photoreduction; staged after aminonitrile formation; anoxic"}, "mechanism": "Sulfide-enabled photoreductive partial hydrolysis converts the nitrile to the corresponding aldehyde with sulfur oxidation.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) show H2S/UV conversion of beta-aminopropionitrile to beta-aminopropionaldehyde. This Cu-free variant is retained because the broader cyanosulfidic system can use sulfide photochemistry without requiring copper at every step, although realistic sulfidic anion availability remains a geochemical constraint (Patel et al., 2015; Ranjan et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Cu-assisted H2S/UV reduction of beta-aminopropionitrile to beta-aminopropionaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_007", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_003", "mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(I)/Cu(II)", "aqueous": true, "other": "UV irradiation; HS-/H2S present; copper-enabled photoredox cycling; anoxic"}, "mechanism": "The same sulfide-driven nitrile-to-aldehyde conversion is accelerated by Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox mediation.", "reasoning": "Copper photoredox accelerates several UV-driven reductions in the cyanosulfidic network, so a Cu-assisted version is chemically justified as an alternative to the slower Cu-free branch (Patel et al., 2015). The same surface constraints on UV availability and sulfur supply apply (Ranjan et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Cyanamide trapping of beta-aminopropionaldehyde to 3-guanidinopropanal", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Freshly generated amino aldehyde intercepted by residual cyanamide; concentrated pool or wet-dry pulse; anoxic"}, "mechanism": "The terminal amine of the amino aldehyde reacts with cyanamide to install the guanidino functionality at the aldehyde stage.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) specifically found aldehyde-stage cyanamide trapping to be more effective than direct guanidination of beta-aminopropionitrile, making this the best-supported branch toward arginine. Cyanamide persistence in water is chemically plausible on prebiotic timescales even though it also participates in competing reactions (Patel et al., 2015; Steinman et al., 1964)."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Cu-free cyanosulfidic homologation of the guanidinated C3 aldehyde to arginine alpha-aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_001", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_007", "mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Net node represents staged use of two cyanide and two sulfide equivalents across cyanohydrin and thioamide chemistry; UV irradiation; anoxic"}, "mechanism": "Net Patel-style sequence of cyanohydrin formation, thioamide generation, UV-driven deoxygenation/reduction, and further homologation to the C5 alpha-aminonitrile precursor.", "reasoning": "This node compresses the experimentally supported Patel arginine branch from the guanidinated C3 aldeh