er\": \"UV-exposed anoxic surface pool; staged cyanide delivery; local cyanide concentration by evaporation or ferrocyanide release is favored\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cu(I)-activated coupling of cyanide-derived carbon with acetylene gives the C3 nitrile acrylonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. identified acrylonitrile as the entry point to the Arg/Pro branch from HCN and acetylene under Cu-mediated cyanosulfidic conditions. A subaerial surface setting is favored because UV is available and cyanide can be locally concentrated and stored in ferrocyanide-bearing basins before release, though that geochemical sequencing is demanding (Patel et al., 2015; Toner and Catling, 2019; Ranjan and Sasselov, 2017).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia addition to acrylonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"298-323K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic aqueous pool with dissolved NH3/NH4+; no molecular oxygen\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia adds across the activated alkene/nitrile system to form beta-aminopropionitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ammonia addition to acrylonitrile to give beta-aminopropionitrile is a core early step of the Patel arginine branch and is chemically straightforward in anoxic water. Among Arg-specific steps, this is relatively well supported experimentally (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu-accelerated HS-/UV conversion of beta-aminopropionitrile to beta-aminopropionaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox species\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"298-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox species\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV irradiation; anoxic HS-/H2S photoredox; sulfur output is a formal S(0)/polysulfide sink\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HS-/UV photoreduction converts the nitrile to an aldehyde-equivalent, with water participation and net ammonia release.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. used sulfide-driven photochemistry to access beta-aminopropionaldehyde from beta-aminopropionitrile. The node is written as the balanced net transformation consuming water and sulfide and releasing NH3 plus elemental sulfur/polysulfide. Copper is treated here as a kinetic accelerator rather than an absolute requirement. Surface sulfide supply is a real geochemical constraint, so this step is strongest in locally sulfidic surface waters (Patel et al., 2015; Ranjan et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Aldehyde-stage cyanamide guanidination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"298-323K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Residual cyanamide present immediately after aldehyde generation; anoxic, concentrated surface pool\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanamide traps the aminoaldehyde and yields a guanidinated aldehyde-equivalent pool represented here by an open-chain network surrogate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The literature favors aldehyde-stage interception by cyanamide over direct guanidination of the aminonitrile. Patel et al. found that residual cyanamide rapidly trapped the photogenerated aminoaldehyde and diverted chemistry toward the arginine branch; this node intentionally represents that equilibrating trapped pool as an open-chain guanidinated aldehyde equivalent rather than claiming a single uniquely isolated species (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct cyanamide guanidination of beta-aminopropionitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"298-323K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated cyanamide; competing unreacted starting material persists\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The terminal amine attacks cyanamide to give a guanidino-functionalized nitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Direct cyanamide guanidination of beta-aminopropionitrile is chemically possible but was relatively inefficient in the Patel study, which left appreciable starting material and cyanamide in the mixture. It is therefore retained only as a lower-flux alternative branch and not as the preferred route (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Speculative HS-/UV conversion of guanidino nitrile to guanidinated aldehyde equivalent\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"298-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV irradiation with dissolved sulfide; included as a low-confidence continuation of the direct guanidination branch\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HS-/UV photoreduction and hydrolytic ammonia loss convert the guanidino nitrile into a guanidinated aldehyde-equivalent.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This step is speculative. The provided literature supports inefficient direct guanidination of beta-aminopropionitrile, but it does not clearly establish this guanidino-nitrile-to-aldehyde sequence as a separately characterized arginine-specific step. It is included because any productive direct nitrile-stage branch must still reach an aldehyde-equivalent before further homologation, and the Patel HS-/UV chemistry makes such a transformation chemically plausible even if less well supported than the aldehyde-first route (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu-accelerated Patel homologation to arginine alpha-aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox species\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"298-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox species\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV-driven staged HCN/HS- additions; net stoichiometry implies 2 HCN and 2 H2S; anoxic surface pool-to-pool sequence\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential cyanohydrin formation, thioamide chemistry, and UV/H2S-driven homologation extend the guanidinated C3 branch to the C6 arginine alpha-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This reaction deliberately compresses several Patel substeps into one balanced network edge; the stoichiometry implies two HCN and two H2S equivalents even though each reactant appears once in the graph. It stands for cyanohydrin formation, thioamide formation, UV/H2S deoxygenation, and final homologation to the experimentally characterized arginine alpha-aminonitrile precursor. Patel et al. supported the alpha-aminonitrile itself, not a quantified isolated free-arginine endpoint. Copper is included here as an accelerator, not as a universally mandatory reagent (Patel et al., 2015; Ranjan et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface hydrolysis of arginine alpha-aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Prolonged aqueous residence or wet-dry rehydration in geothermal surface pools; balanced form requires 2 H2O\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aqueous hydrolysis of the alpha-aminonitrile gives the amino acid and releases ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydrolysis of an alpha-aminonitrile to the corresponding amino acid is the expected Strecker endpoint; the balanced form of this node requires two waters and releases NH3. For arginine specifically, Patel et al. established the aminonitrile precursor rather than free arginine as the headline product, so this step is chemically plausible but less directly demonstrated than formation of mol_013 (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Mineral-interface hydrolysis of arginine alpha-aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Post-photochemical transfer to concentrated prebiotic pools or mineral interfaces; balanced form requires 2 H2O\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Slow aqueous nitrile hydrolysis proceeds after the photochemical construction stage, allowing environmental succession between modules.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The same hydrolysis chemistry could occur after transfer of the aminonitrile into concentrated prebiotic pools or mineral interfaces, separating UV construction from slower nitrile hydrolysis. This environmental handoff is plausible but not directly validated for arginine, so the step is explicitly more speculative than the formation of the aminonitrile precursor itself (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfide-only HS-/UV conversion of beta-aminopropionitrile to beta-aminopropionaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"298-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV irradiation with dissolved HS-/H2S but no explicit copper accelerator; sulfur output is a formal S(0)/polysulfide sink\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sulfide photoreduction alone converts the nitrile to the aldehyde-equivalent with hydrolytic ammonia release.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"A Cu-free sulfide-photoredox variant is included because Patel's broader cyanosulfidic system does not require copper for every reduction, although copper can accelerate photoredox turnover. This version is chemically defensible but likely slower and more sensitive to local sulfur availability than rxn_003 (Patel et al., 2015; Ranjan et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfide-only Patel homologation to arginine alpha-aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"298-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV-driven staged HCN/HS- additions without explicit copper acceleration; net stoichiometry implies 2 HCN and 2 H2S\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The same HCN/thioamide/homologation logic as rxn_007 operates without copper acceleration.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the Cu-free analogue of rxn_007. It represents the same Patel-type HCN/H2S homologation logic but without invoking copper acceleration, which is chemically plausible in parts of the cyanosulfidic network yet likely kinetically less favorable. As with rxn_007, the secure literature endpoint is the arginine alpha-aminonitrile rather than free arginine (Patel et al., 2015; Ranjan et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanamide guanidination of ornithine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated cyanamide in mildly basic to neutral prebiotic pool; no mineral catalyst required\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The terminal delta-amine of ornithine attacks the electrophilic carbon of cyanamide and tautomerizes to the guanidino group of arginine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Terminal amine guanidination is the chemically shortest late-stage route from a C5 skeleton to arginine. Longo et al. showed complete Orn->Arg conversion for peptide-bound ornithine under aqueous guanidination conditions, and cyanamide is a plausible simple prebiotic guanidinating reagent from classic chemical-evolution work. However, a fully naturalistic free-ornithine-plus-cyanamide environmental synthesis of free arginine remains underconstrained, and ornithine supply is itself a bottleneck despite abiotic ornithine evidence from volcanic discharge chemistry (Longo et al., 2020; Steinman et al., 1964; Johnson et al., 2008).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Preferred Patel branch with surface hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Most evidence-weighted de novo route: Cu-mediated entry chemistry, Cu-accelerated HS-/UV aldehyde formation, aldehyde-stage cyanamide interception, Cu-accelerated homologation to the alpha-aminonitrile, then surface hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Preferred Patel branch with post-transfer hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Same chemically preferred de novo construction as P1, but free arginine appears only after transfer of the alpha-aminonitrile to a concentrated prebiotic pool or mineral interface for hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Minimal-copper aldehyde-generation variant\",\n      \"description\": \"Aldehyde-stage guanidination is retained, but the nitrile-to-aldehyde step is run by sulfide photochemistry without explicit copper acceleration.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Minimal-copper aldehyde-generation with post-transfer hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"As in P3, but hydrolysis of the alpha-aminonitrile is deferred to a later concentrated pool or mineral-interface stage.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_0